::PC_U01_UA_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.7
Question:
Which function has the lower minimum: f(x)=x^2+2 or g(x)=x^2-5?
{
=A. g(x)#g has minimum -5, which is lower than 2.
~B. f(x)#This reverses the minimum comparison.
~C. both are equal#The vertical shifts make different minimums.
~D. neither has a minimum#Both upward parabolas have minimums.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
What is the degree of p(x)=3x^5-2x+1?
{
~A. 3#This is the leading coefficient, not degree.
=B. 5#The highest exponent is 5.
~C. 2#This is a lower exponent.
~D. 1#This is the constant term degree misconception.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which zero comes from the factor x-7?
{
~A. -7#This uses the wrong sign.
~B. 0#The zero is not automatically 0.
=C. 7#Setting x-7=0 gives x=7.
~D. 1#This value does not make the factor zero.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.3
Question:
What does a real zero of a polynomial represent on the graph?
{
~A. domain endpoint#A zero is not necessarily a domain endpoint.
~B. y-intercept#A y-intercept occurs where x=0.
~C. vertical asymptote#Polynomial zeros do not create vertical asymptotes.
=D. x-intercept#A real zero is where the graph crosses or touches the x-axis.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What is the domain of sqrt(x+6)?
{
=A. x >= -6#The radicand x+6 must be nonnegative.
~B. x <= -6#This reverses the inequality.
~C. x cannot equal -6#x=-6 is allowed because the radicand is 0.
~D. all real numbers#The square root restricts the domain.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which value is excluded from 1/(x-4)?
{
~A. -4#This uses the wrong sign.
=B. 4#The denominator is zero when x=4.
~C. 0#The denominator is not zero at 0.
~D. 1#This does not make the denominator zero.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.8
Question:
Let f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=3x. What is (f+g)(x)?
{
~A. x+5#This combines unlike terms incorrectly.
~B. 3x^2+6x#This multiplies functions instead of adding.
=C. 4x+2#Add outputs: x+2+3x=4x+2.
~D. 3x+2#This omits the x from f(x).
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.9
Question:
Let f(x)=x^2 and g(x)=x+3. What is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x^2+3#This does not square the full inner expression.
~B. 2x+3#This treats the square as multiplying by 2.
~C. x^2+6#This expands incorrectly.
=D. (x+3)^2#Substitute x+3 into f and square the entire expression.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q09::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.10
Question:
If distance depends on time and cost depends on distance, what does cost(distance(time)) represent?
{
=A. cost from time#The inner function gives distance from time, then cost from distance.
~B. time from cost#This reverses the output relationship.
~C. distance from cost#This reverses the composition.
~D. cost plus distance#Composition is not addition.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q10::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x)=x-6?
{
~A. x-6#This repeats the original function.
=B. x+6#Adding 6 undoes subtracting 6.
~C. 6x#Multiplication does not undo subtraction.
~D. x/6#Division does not undo subtraction.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q11::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.12
Question:
Why restrict f(x)=x^2 to x>=0 before finding an inverse?
{
~A. to make it linear#The function remains quadratic.
~B. to remove all outputs#The function still has outputs.
=C. to make it one-to-one#Restricting to one side makes the function pass the horizontal line test.
~D. to make the range negative#The range is not made negative.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q12::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.13
Question:
A function converts feet to meters. What does its inverse do?
{
~A. feet plus meters#An inverse is not addition.
~B. feet to feet#This does not reverse the relationship.
~C. meters to meters#This keeps the output unit.
=D. meters to feet#The inverse reverses the conversion.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q13::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which model represents 4 percent growth from 250?
{
=A. 250(1.04)^t#Growth by 4 percent uses factor 1.04.
~B. 250(0.96)^t#This represents 4 percent decay.
~C. 4(250)^t#This switches initial amount and factor.
~D. 250+4t#This is linear, not exponential.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q14::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What is log base 3 of 81?
{
~A. 3#3^3=27, not 81.
=B. 4#3^4=81.
~C. 27#This is not the exponent.
~D. 78#This subtracts values.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q15::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.MOD.1
Question:
Solve y=x+1 and y=6.
{
~A. (1,6)#This uses the added constant as x.
~B. (6,5)#This reverses coordinates.
=C. (5,6)#x+1=6 gives x=5.
~D. (7,6)#This adds 1 to 6.
}
::PC_U01_UA_Q16::
Question ID: PC_U01_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.MOD.2
Question:
For f(x)=2x when x<0 and f(x)=x^2 when x>=0, what is f(3)?
{
~A. 6#This uses the wrong interval rule.
~B. 0#This is the boundary output misconception.
~C. 3#This repeats the input.
=D. 9#Since 3>=0, use x^2.
}
